WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.858
<v Emeka>Go on, tap the table. Keep going. Feel that in your arm?

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.782
<v Bev>I can feel it in my shoulder.

00:00:06.758 --> 00:00:07.649
<v Emeka>That's WOLF.

00:00:13.449 --> 00:00:17.406
<v Gareth>That's WOLF loading. The game that made Emeka's arms ache.

00:00:22.406 --> 00:00:29.627
<v Bev>Hello there, it's Bev, the kettle's on, and this is Dalex After Hours, the slot where we find out how computer games are really made.

00:00:30.227 --> 00:00:33.735
<v Bev>Every fact on this show comes out of the book the team's writing.

00:00:34.335 --> 00:00:35.846
<v Bev>Across the desk, Gareth.

00:00:35.926 --> 00:00:36.678
<v Gareth>Hiya, Bev.

00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:48.966
<v Bev>And this week we're on the floor. Literally, because our guest sits on a big red beanbag. He's the playtester, the best player in the building, and he's brought a joystick. Emeka Ezeani.

00:00:49.141 --> 00:00:51.430
<v Emeka>Hiya. It's a very comfy beanbag.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.611
<v Bev>And you look very happy.

00:00:52.811 --> 00:01:03.189
<v Emeka>Ten one, Bev. City beat Huddersfield ten one on Saturday. Three hat-tricks, Paul Stewart, Tony Adcock and David White. I've been playing it back in my head all week.

00:01:03.421 --> 00:01:09.993
<v Gareth>And six two against Plymouth on Tuesday. Sixteen goals in two home games. Somebody check the scoreboard.

00:01:10.142 --> 00:01:11.738
<v Emeka>I'd give that scoreboard an A.

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:14.538
<v Bev>Is it true you waggle the joystick even in the menus?

00:01:14.605 --> 00:01:15.869
<v Emeka>Keeps the wrist warm.

00:01:15.995 --> 00:01:17.392
<v Gareth>He's always beating my high score.

00:01:17.414 --> 00:01:18.581
<v Emeka>Beat your high score.

00:01:18.596 --> 00:01:19.554
<v Gareth>Impossible.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.889
<v Emeka>Screen four.

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:24.327
<v Bev>I don't know what that means and I can tell I'm not meant to.

00:01:27.627 --> 00:01:31.187
<v Bev>Go on then, Emeka, your desk. Except you haven't got one.

00:01:31.397 --> 00:01:38.234
<v Emeka>I've got a telly. The playtest telly. Every verified tape goes in the Spectrum under it, and I play it properly.

00:01:38.205 --> 00:01:39.971
<v Bev>Properly meaning what?

00:01:40.336 --> 00:01:44.918
<v Emeka>Meaning I play it like somebody who's paid for it. And I write down everything that annoys me.

00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:45.797
<v Bev>Everything?

00:01:46.038 --> 00:01:56.412
<v Emeka>Everything. If something annoys me, I write it down there and then, mid-round. Then it gets a grade. It's always about the game, never the people who made it.

00:01:56.624 --> 00:02:00.214
<v Gareth>He writes them very kindly. And every one makes the game better.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.967
<v Bev>And who brings you the tapes?

00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:06.134
<v Emeka>Omnibot brings me every verified test copy. And keeps the receipt.

00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:12.130
<v Bev>And there he is now, rolling past the beanbag with a cassette. Perfect timing, for once.

00:02:12.407 --> 00:02:14.732
<v Gareth>Don't tell him. He'll get ideas.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.667
<v Bev>And the cat's asleep by your telly.

00:02:16.797 --> 00:02:21.613
<v Emeka>Percy's bed is by my telly. He's my best mate. He knows a good game.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.291
<v Bev>So what's WOLF, for anyone who hasn't played it?

00:02:24.678 --> 00:02:36.806
<v Emeka>You're Dex. He's forty-nine, and he's the newest member of the WOLF Pack, a gym for men over thirty-five. You go round the stations, you lift, you drink at the water cooler, and you try to raise your numbers on the board.

00:02:36.843 --> 00:02:38.032
<v Bev>And can you lose?

00:02:38.386 --> 00:02:41.378
<v Emeka>There's no game over. Just Dex, a bit out of breath.

00:02:41.526 --> 00:02:50.845
<v Gareth>It's an affectionate in-joke about a real gym, made to be shown to the people who inspired it. Every coach is competent and likeable, and every joke is on Dex's own laziness.

00:02:50.824 --> 00:02:53.354
<v Bev>That's nice, that. Poor Dex.

00:02:53.492 --> 00:02:56.169
<v Emeka>Dex is fine. Dex is resting.

00:02:56.328 --> 00:02:59.198
<v Bev>Right then. The one idea this week.

00:02:59.372 --> 00:03:02.015
<v Emeka>Timing you can feel has to be measured every frame.

00:03:02.293 --> 00:03:05.151
<v Bev>Every frame. Remind me what a frame is again.

00:03:05.294 --> 00:03:12.976
<v Gareth>The telly draws a fresh picture fifty times a second. That's a frame. One every twenty thousandths of a second.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.938
<v Bev>So that's the game's clock.

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:21.802
<v Gareth>That's the only clock it's got. A Spectrum game doesn't ask what time it is. It counts frames.

00:03:22.021 --> 00:03:24.286
<v Bev>So how do you lift a bar in WOLF?

00:03:24.558 --> 00:03:37.346
<v Emeka>You tap O and P in turn. O, P, O, P. Like pedalling with two fingers. Keep a steady rhythm and the bar climbs and the rep counts. Then you press space as the top lamp lights, and again at the bottom.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.214
<v Bev>And if you just hammer the keys?

00:03:39.497 --> 00:03:43.840
<v Emeka>Then it doesn't. That's the whole game. Rhythm wins.

00:03:44.058 --> 00:03:46.376
<v Bev>So how does it know what a steady rhythm is?

00:03:46.674 --> 00:03:53.210
<v Emeka>It doesn't look at the taps. It looks at the gaps between them. A drummer who keeps time leaves the same gap every beat.

00:03:53.397 --> 00:04:03.475
<v Gareth>And every gap comes out as a whole number of frames. A stopwatch might say nought point one three seven of a second. A frame counter says seven. Never six and a bit.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:04.988
<v Bev>So what's a good gap?

00:04:05.305 --> 00:04:10.070
<v Emeka>Six to ten frames between taps. That's five to a little over eight taps a second.

00:04:10.347 --> 00:04:13.548
<v Bev>Eight taps a second. My arm's aching already.

00:04:13.582 --> 00:04:14.559
<v Emeka>That's the game working.

00:04:14.608 --> 00:04:16.230
<v Bev>So what's my homework?

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:28.940
<v Emeka>You need a digital watch with a stopwatch that does laps. Borrow Vik's, if he'll let you. Start it with your first tap, then press the lap button five more times, steady, as fast as you could keep up for a minute.

00:04:29.087 --> 00:04:32.962
<v Bev>As fast as I could keep up for a minute. That's not very fast.

00:04:33.083 --> 00:04:42.276
<v Emeka>That's fine. Dex is the same. Then read off the five lap times, multiply each by fifty and round it. That's each gap in frames.

00:04:42.472 --> 00:04:54.230
<v Gareth>The book has a worked answer. Laps of nought point one six, nought point one four, nought point one eight, nought point one six and nought point two seconds. Times fifty, that's eight, seven, nine, eight and ten frames.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.587
<v Bev>And are those good?

00:04:55.970 --> 00:05:01.344
<v Emeka>Every one's inside six to ten. Good rhythm, a little uneven, the way a real person always is.

00:05:01.595 --> 00:05:03.168
<v Bev>And if mine came out under six?

00:05:03.448 --> 00:05:06.477
<v Emeka>You were rushing. Over ten, you were lazing.

00:05:06.662 --> 00:05:08.537
<v Gareth>And Dex would be a relaxed lifter.

00:05:08.689 --> 00:05:10.985
<v Bev>I'll have a go. Pause me, everyone.

00:05:14.585 --> 00:05:18.768
<v Bev>Right. I got nine, eleven, ten, twelve and ten.

00:05:18.991 --> 00:05:27.692
<v Emeka>Relaxed. Very relaxed. Nine and the tens are in the band. The eleven and the twelve are over ten, so that's you lazing.

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:28.978
<v Bev>I prefer resting.

00:05:29.191 --> 00:05:33.766
<v Emeka>Dex says resting too. Keep that paper, though, because there's a story about relaxed coming up.

00:05:34.048 --> 00:05:41.609
<v Gareth>And notice what you did there, Bev. You didn't time the taps. You timed the gaps. That's exactly what the game does.

00:05:41.689 --> 00:05:43.343
<v Bev>So I'm a computer now.

00:05:43.537 --> 00:05:47.351
<v Gareth>A very relaxed one. The game looks fifty times a second.

00:05:49.851 --> 00:05:53.210
<v Bev>So that's WOLF playing now. What's going on underneath?

00:05:53.527 --> 00:06:03.360
<v Gareth>Here's the catch. The main program in WOLF only runs once every four frames, because drawing the gym takes that long. Twelve and a half times a second.

00:06:03.525 --> 00:06:07.498
<v Bev>Four frames. Wasn't that the same as the jungle game, last week?

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:19.545
<v Gareth>Good memory. It's the period Tiger Gorilla settled on too. The team first planned three frames for WOLF, and measured a camera step. It came out far over what three frames give you, so it became four.

00:06:19.737 --> 00:06:21.681
<v Bev>So the gym is heavy to draw.

00:06:21.838 --> 00:06:27.474
<v Emeka>The gym's heavy to draw. The bar's heavy. Everything's heavy in WOLF.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:28.596
<v Bev>Is that a problem?

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:36.110
<v Gareth>For the lift it would be. If the main program did the measuring, it could only say which four frames a tap landed in, not which frame.

00:06:36.146 --> 00:06:37.516
<v Bev>So it rounds.

00:06:37.722 --> 00:06:49.103
<v Gareth>It rounds, and badly. Take your worked taps. The real gaps were eight, seven, nine, eight and ten. Counted every four frames, the game would see eight, four, twelve, eight and eight.

00:06:49.118 --> 00:06:51.495
<v Bev>Twelve? I didn't tap twelve.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:55.957
<v Gareth>You didn't. And two of those five are outside the band altogether.

00:06:56.088 --> 00:07:06.048
<v Emeka>And here's the bit I care about. Take a player who's perfectly steady, a tap every six frames, bang on. Counted every four frames, the game sees four, eight, four, eight.

00:07:06.315 --> 00:07:08.268
<v Bev>So the steady one looks wobbly.

00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:13.502
<v Emeka>The steady one looks like he's lurching. And he'd be marked down for a rhythm that felt spot on.

00:07:13.786 --> 00:07:20.165
<v Gareth>Of the whole good band, six to ten, counting every four frames can only ever see one value. Eight.

00:07:20.443 --> 00:07:22.409
<v Bev>So it cheats the careful players.

00:07:22.623 --> 00:07:34.726
<v Emeka>It cheats the careful ones hardest. And that's not fair. Think of a high-score table in an arcade. Nobody minds losing to a better player. Everyone minds losing to someone who found a trick.

00:07:34.892 --> 00:07:36.167
<v Bev>So how does WOLF do it?

00:07:36.452 --> 00:07:47.790
<v Gareth>The whole lift runs in the interrupt. That's the hardware tapping the processor on the shoulder as each picture starts. It stops what it's doing, runs a short routine, and goes back to exactly where it was.

00:07:48.016 --> 00:07:50.354
<v Bev>Like the doorbell going while you're cooking.

00:07:50.503 --> 00:08:02.353
<v Gareth>That's how the book puts it. Only this doorbell rings fifty times a second, on the dot. And it reads both keys with one instruction, I N, from one port. P and O are on the same half row of the keyboard.

00:08:02.443 --> 00:08:04.369
<v Bev>Why does one instruction matter?

00:08:04.706 --> 00:08:12.912
<v Gareth>Because no press can sneak in between two reads and look like the wrong order. Then it keeps last frame's keys, and a press is a key that's down now and was up then.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:14.808
<v Bev>So if I hold P down?

00:08:15.158 --> 00:08:18.235
<v Emeka>One press, then nothing. Holding does nothing.

00:08:18.266 --> 00:08:19.550
<v Bev>And both keys at once?

00:08:19.797 --> 00:08:32.103
<v Emeka>Ignored. And the same key twice is ignored. After P it wants O, after O it wants P. Hold both keys, then hammer one. A new player tries that in their first round, before anyone's told them the rhythm.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:46.242
<v Gareth>Vik's test rig has a recipe that does exactly that. The rig plays the tape and presses keys at exact moments. It holds O and P together for a hundred and fifty frames, then P alone for a hundred, and expects the bar never to pass four of its sixty-three steps.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:47.620
<v Bev>And the main program?

00:08:47.916 --> 00:08:54.547
<v Gareth>It only draws what the interrupt publishes. The lift costs about four thousand, five hundred clock ticks per update, measured.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:58.499
<v Bev>And what if I'm a little bit off? Nobody taps like a metronome.

00:08:58.757 --> 00:09:08.168
<v Emeka>That's the nice part. The game keeps a running average of your recent gaps and judges each one against that. A gap within three frames of your average costs nothing at all.

00:09:08.391 --> 00:09:13.093
<v Gareth>The book calls that a dead zone. Like the slack in a steering wheel before the wheels turn.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:14.763
<v Bev>So a wobble's forgiven.

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:19.163
<v Emeka>A wobble of a frame either way costs nothing. Any steady rhythm in the band is clean.

00:09:19.997 --> 00:09:22.455
<v Bev>So what happens when I'm more than a wobble out?

00:09:22.844 --> 00:09:32.188
<v Emeka>Then the form meter moves. It's a running score of how cleanly you're lifting. Rushing pushes it one way, ragged pushes it the other, and good pressing settles it back to the middle.

00:09:32.267 --> 00:09:33.544
<v Bev>Like a spirit level.

00:09:33.824 --> 00:09:37.494
<v Emeka>Bit like that. Go too far either way and that rep's spoiled.

00:09:37.618 --> 00:09:39.877
<v Bev>Give me a round, then. A real one.

00:09:40.036 --> 00:09:47.393
<v Emeka>Right. Picture a player who goes short, long, short, long. A gap of five, then eleven, then five, then eleven.

00:09:47.394 --> 00:09:48.406
<v Bev>Lurching.

00:09:48.571 --> 00:10:00.605
<v Emeka>Lurching. The first gaps cost him nothing while his average settles. Then on the fourth gap, the eleven, he's way past the dead zone, and the meter jumps right off the ragged side. That rep's spoiled.

00:10:00.749 --> 00:10:02.736
<v Bev>And somebody going six, ten, six, ten?

00:10:03.095 --> 00:10:07.831
<v Emeka>Takes one small knock while the average is still settling, then settles back to nothing. Clean.

00:10:08.019 --> 00:10:11.291
<v Bev>But that's still uneven. Why's one fine and the other not?

00:10:11.628 --> 00:10:24.428
<v Gareth>Because the team set the dead zone by arithmetic, to land between them. Make it two and a quarter frames and the six and ten player fails, and that rhythm has to stay clean. Make it three and three quarters and the lurching five and eleven passes.

00:10:24.607 --> 00:10:27.518
<v Bev>So three frames is the gap between the two.

00:10:27.531 --> 00:10:29.410
<v Gareth>Three frames sits between them.

00:10:29.363 --> 00:10:30.284
<v Bev>And the masher?

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:36.089
<v Emeka>A masher at a gap of three climbs steadily up the rushing side until he crosses the edge, on the sixth gap.

00:10:36.313 --> 00:10:38.026
<v Bev>Did it always work like this?

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:50.541
<v Gareth>No. The first design took the rate from taps counted over a window of twenty-five frames. It was dropped, because a count like that moves in coarse steps of two presses a second, and it lags by the length of its own window.

00:10:50.685 --> 00:10:54.761
<v Emeka>You'd feel that. You'd speed up and the bar wouldn't notice straight away.

00:10:54.896 --> 00:10:57.242
<v Bev>So now every gap, every frame.

00:10:57.525 --> 00:11:04.142
<v Gareth>Every gap, every frame. That's the rule of the chapter. Timing a player can feel belongs to the frame.

00:11:04.412 --> 00:11:08.337
<v Bev>Go on then. You said there was a story about relaxed.

00:11:08.491 --> 00:11:13.419
<v Emeka>There are two, and they're my favourite kind, because no automatic check found them. Players did.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:14.004
<v Bev>Go on.

00:11:14.192 --> 00:11:19.686
<v Emeka>Settle into a steady gap of ten. That's the most relaxed rhythm in the band. Same as yours, Bev.

00:11:19.751 --> 00:11:20.642
<v Bev>My people.

00:11:20.911 --> 00:11:29.033
<v Emeka>The bar rises nice and even to sixty of its sixty-three steps. And then it stops. Bar stalled. Too heavy.

00:11:29.247 --> 00:11:34.995
<v Bev>Oh, poor Dex. Three steps from the top.

00:11:35.149 --> 00:11:40.340
<v Emeka>At the start weight that happened three sets out of three. A gap of ten never finished a rep.

00:11:40.557 --> 00:11:46.336
<v Gareth>And INK and PAPER's review put it plainly. The comfy rhythm you settle into is the one that fails.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:47.514
<v Bev>But you'd tested it?

00:11:47.777 --> 00:12:01.144
<v Gareth>The fairness check on Vik's rig tests each rule press by press. It never asked whether a set at a gap of ten finishes. And the recipes did play a wobble round ten, but against a light thirty kilo weight, not the fifty-five kilo start weight.

00:12:01.394 --> 00:12:02.917
<v Bev>So they never met the stall.

00:12:03.217 --> 00:12:07.250
<v Emeka>Never. It took players to show that was the question to ask.

00:12:07.325 --> 00:12:09.029
<v Bev>So what did you do?

00:12:09.409 --> 00:12:23.365
<v Emeka>The ruling was simple. Any steady rhythm in the band must lift the start weight. The sag that makes a heavy bar slow came down. By the team's estimate, a gap of ten now tops a rep in about a hundred and sixty-five of the two hundred frames allowed.

00:12:23.427 --> 00:12:24.760
<v Bev>And the second story?

00:12:25.136 --> 00:12:32.074
<v Emeka>Mashing. Hammering the keys at a gap of three. It gave five scrappy reps in fifteen point eight seconds.

00:12:32.081 --> 00:12:33.229
<v Bev>Is that good?

00:12:33.475 --> 00:12:35.949
<v Emeka>A clean set at a gap of eight took twenty point four.

00:12:36.221 --> 00:12:38.108
<v Bev>So the masher got off the rack first.

00:12:38.326 --> 00:12:47.176
<v Emeka>He earned nothing, no good reps, but he got off the rack sooner. And if a trick gets you home early, players will find it. The playtests caught it.

00:12:47.426 --> 00:12:57.847
<v Gareth>So the fix makes the bar's speed peak inside the band. If you go faster than a gap of six, your speed mirrors back round it. A masher at a gap of three lifts like a gentle nine.

00:12:57.934 --> 00:12:59.892
<v Bev>So hammering slows you down.

00:13:00.082 --> 00:13:03.386
<v Emeka>Hammering is never the quick way through a set. Now rhythm wins.

00:13:03.643 --> 00:13:07.437
<v Gareth>And that was the playtests' catch, start to finish. Emeka's trade.

00:13:07.583 --> 00:13:10.729
<v Emeka>The whole team fixed it, though. I only wrote it down.

00:13:10.997 --> 00:13:15.288
<v Bev>And the space bar? I've got a nephew who'd tape it down.

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:32.814
<v Emeka>Autofire. A joystick setting that presses fire again and again by itself. That's covered. Every frame the game stamps whether space went down into a little ring of thirty-two slots. At the lamp it counts the presses. Exactly one is good. Two is a miss, straight away.

00:13:32.968 --> 00:13:40.566
<v Gareth>Counted every four frames, those two presses would have merged into one and slipped through. Stamped every frame, they can't.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:42.224
<v Bev>So the robot loses.

00:13:42.483 --> 00:13:47.436
<v Emeka>The robot loses. Omnibot doesn't mind. He only carries the tapes.

00:13:47.562 --> 00:13:50.024
<v Bev>And is it right now? How do you know?

00:13:50.320 --> 00:14:03.850
<v Gareth>Vik's rig has twenty checks of the lift's rules. At milestone five it passes all twenty, with a hundred and fifty of a hundred and fifty beat verdicts right. And presses jittered a frame round gaps of six, eight and ten give a hundred per cent clean reps.

00:14:04.149 --> 00:14:06.925
<v Bev>A hundred per cent. Vik would like that.

00:14:07.210 --> 00:14:09.714
<v Gareth>Vik would want to see it run. Fair enough.

00:14:09.905 --> 00:14:14.495
<v Bev>Go on, Gareth, crystal ball time. Will games still be counting frames in the future?

00:14:14.757 --> 00:14:26.826
<v Gareth>Ooh. Suppose one day there's a language, call it Python, after Monty Python. You'd ask the computer what time it is, to a millionth of a second, at each key press, and take one from the other.

00:14:26.786 --> 00:14:28.278
<v Bev>Like on Tomorrow's World.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:34.204
<v Emeka>And suppose everybody carries a telephone in their pocket, with a stopwatch in it. You'd do Bev's homework on the bus.

00:14:34.411 --> 00:14:37.507
<v Bev>A telephone in your pocket. Where would you plug it in?

00:14:37.675 --> 00:14:39.935
<v Emeka>No idea. It's a guess.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:45.309
<v Gareth>And I'd guess the games would still look at the gaps, not the taps. A drummer's a drummer, whatever the clock.

00:14:45.521 --> 00:14:47.729
<v Bev>Ask us again in thirty years.

00:14:48.329 --> 00:14:50.417
<v Bev>So what should folk try before next week?

00:14:50.770 --> 00:15:00.197
<v Gareth>In the book the team's writing, chapter thirteen is WOLF: Timing, Waggling and Scripted Characters. The first exercise is Sample the pump on paper.

00:15:00.276 --> 00:15:01.006
<v Bev>Pencil job?

00:15:01.278 --> 00:15:17.317
<v Gareth>Pencil job. Take a player tapping every seven frames. Write down the frame of each tap, then work out which four-frame window it lands in, and the gaps that window count would see. Then do it again for ten. Compare the worst mistake with the three-frame dead zone.

00:15:17.479 --> 00:15:22.975
<v Emeka>And then go and play it with a gap of ten and feel the bar get to the top. That's the fix, in your arm.

00:15:23.132 --> 00:15:29.752
<v Gareth>And the same chapter goes on to the coaches, who run from little scripts kept as data. That's another week. Go on then.

00:15:33.752 --> 00:15:38.922
<v Bev>That's us for this week. Thank you, Emeka. My arm will never be the same.

00:15:39.099 --> 00:15:43.549
<v Emeka>Thanks, Bev. Thanks, Gareth. Screen four.

00:15:43.564 --> 00:15:44.591
<v Gareth>I'm practising.

00:15:44.869 --> 00:15:54.472
<v Bev>Next week, Derek's in. He keeps the gate, and nothing leaves the building until he's signed the sheet. If you've got a question for the team, send it in and we'll put it to them.

00:15:54.533 --> 00:15:55.623
<v Emeka>One more go.

00:15:55.676 --> 00:15:56.907
<v Bev>Right then, turn it up.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:09.736
<v Daniel>This is Daniel Vaughan, who made the show. That was nineteen eighty-seven. Gareth's guess holds up: in Python three you can read the clock at each key press and take one from the other. The chapter compares that with counting frames.

00:16:10.336 --> 00:16:21.950
<v Daniel>You can play WOLF free in your browser at dalex-software dot co dot uk. For the other exercises you'll want an emulator such as Fuse, the sjasmplus assembler and Python three.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:31.212
<v Daniel>The chapter is WOLF: Timing, Waggling and Scripted Characters, in Machine Code Mayhem on Leanpub, and the first exercise is Sample the pump on paper.

00:16:31.812 --> 00:16:46.513
<v Daniel>Thank you for listening. The theme is by Dez, the characters are fictional, the voices are synthesised, and the show is written and produced by me, Daniel Vaughan, with AI agents, from my book Machine Code Mayhem. The games are real, and so is every fact.
